# One identifier spelling exempted from readability-identifier-naming, inside
# the bank example's own headers: `TableName`, the member name the LASTRADA
# Lightweight ORM reads a record's SQL table off. Nothing else is subtracted --
# not a check, not a declaration kind, not a directory's worth of code.
#
# ── What fires, and why the check's own fix is wrong here ────────────────────
#
# The repository-root .clang-tidy sets `readability-identifier-naming.
# VariableCase: camelBack`, and a Lightweight record declares
#
#     static constexpr std::string_view TableName = "morph_offline_queue";
#
# which clang-tidy 22.1.8 reports as
#
#     examples/bank/include/bank/offline/lightweight_offline_queue.hpp:52:39:
#       error: invalid case style for variable 'TableName'
#       [readability-identifier-naming,-warnings-as-errors]
#
# The name is not ours to pick. Lightweight hard-codes it, by spelling, in
# `Lightweight/Utils.hpp`'s `RecordTableNameImpl`:
#
#     if constexpr (requires { Record::TableName; })
#         return Record::TableName;
#     else
#         ... Reflection::TypeNameOf<Record> ...   // the C++ type's own name
#
# So the rename the check asks for is not merely unavailable -- it is silently
# wrong. `tableName` does not fail to compile and does not fail to link: the
# `requires` clause goes false, the `else` branch runs, and the record is
# mapped to a table named after the C++ type instead. A record renamed to
# satisfy a lint writes to the wrong table, and the first thing that tells
# anyone is a missing table at run time.
#
# ── Why an ignore-by-name, and not `-readability-identifier-naming` ──────────
#
# Subtracting the check for a directory would stop it measuring every other
# identifier there, which is the failure mode AGENTS.md names first. An
# `IgnoredRegexp` subtracts a spelling instead, and that claim is measured
# rather than asserted. A probe header placed beside the queue, carrying three
# findings -- `TableName`, a second static constexpr member `OtherThing`, and a
# local `BadLocal` -- analysed with clang-tidy 22.1.8 against this repository's
# real configuration:
#
#   no config here                    TableName, OtherThing, BadLocal reported
#   VariableIgnoredRegexp             OtherThing, BadLocal still reported
#   ClassConstantIgnoredRegexp        only BadLocal -- OtherThing went quiet
#   ConstexprVariableIgnoredRegexp    only BadLocal -- OtherThing went quiet
#
# The last two lines are why the key below is `VariableIgnoredRegexp` and not
# either of the two that read as more specific. clang-tidy picks a
# declaration's naming style by walking a list of candidate kinds and taking
# the first that has *any* option configured. Naming `ClassConstant` or
# `ConstexprVariable` at all -- even only to set its IgnoredRegexp -- moves the
# declaration onto a kind with no `Case` configured, so every static constexpr
# member in reach stops being case-checked whatever it is called. That is a
# gate that stops measuring, wearing the narrower option's clothes. `Variable`
# is the kind the diagnostic actually names ("invalid case style for
# *variable*"), so setting its IgnoredRegexp changes nothing but the one
# spelling.
#
# Not a NOLINT either, though the sibling translation unit
# (examples/bank/src/offline/lightweight_offline_queue.cpp) uses one for
# LIGHTWEIGHT_SQL_MIGRATION and argues there for a call-site suppression over a
# directory-scoped one. The two cases differ in the way that argument turns on.
# That NOLINTBEGIN subtracts five whole checks, so confining it to the macro's
# three lines is what keeps it from covering unrelated code; there is exactly
# one migration in that file and nothing to generalise. `TableName` is the
# opposite shape: twelve declarations in this directory tree today, one per
# record, and what is subtracted is a spelling rather than a check -- so a
# per-line directive would have to be repeated twelve times to say what one key
# says once, and each copy would be a place for the reason to go stale.
#
# ── Why this directory, and no wider or narrower ─────────────────────────────
#
# clang-tidy resolves configuration by walking up from the file it is
# analysing and offers no finer granularity than a directory, so the only
# choice is which directory.
#
# `examples/bank/include/` is the bank example's own header tree and holds no
# .cpp at all, so this file governs declarations and nothing else. Twelve of
# the repository's 71 `static constexpr std::string_view TableName`
# declarations are under it -- eleven across nine files in `bank/db/`, and the
# queue's one in `bank/offline/` -- and they are every one of the bank
# example's. Measured with clang-tidy 22.1.8 on this revision, each of the
# twelve is reported without this file and clean with it. The other four
# subdirectories (`app/`, `core/`, `dto/`, `models/`) are in reach and contain
# no `TableName` to exempt; the ignore has nothing to do there and does
# nothing.
#
# One directory up, at `examples/`, is not available and should not be: a
# config there governs every rung's src/, include/ and gui_lib/ together, which
# is the shape in which a narrow suppression quietly becomes a wide one. That
# holds regardless of how narrow this key is -- the file would be reaching seven
# rungs' production code on the strength of an argument made about the bank
# example. Nothing gates that placement any more, so it is a rule to keep by
# hand.
#
# That leaves the other 59 declarations -- 13 in crm's `db/`, 12 in ledger's,
# 11 in kanban's, 9 in lims', 6 in polls', 5 in bookmarks', 1 in pastebin's,
# and 2 in examples/common/testkit/ -- unexempted and, today, unreported:
# clang-tidy-diff analyses only changed lines, so a declaration nobody has
# touched is never judged. They are latent, not fixed, and
# deliberately left to the rung that owns each one rather than settled from
# here. The two under examples/common/testkit/ are the sharpest of them,
# because they sit in .cpp files that do have compile commands in this job's
# database: editing either of those lines fails clang-tidy-diff the same way
# this header did.
#
# `InheritParentConfig: true` is load-bearing and not decoration. Without it
# clang-tidy *replaces* the root configuration rather than extending it, and
# this file's `CheckOptions` alone enable nothing -- the whole check list gone.
#
# Dropping the key fails loudly rather than silently, which is worth knowing
# before reasoning about whether some other config needs protecting. Measured
# with clang-tidy 22.1.8 and the same compile database as the probe above:
#
#     $ sed -i 's/^InheritParentConfig: true$/# &/' examples/bank/include/.clang-tidy
#     $ clang-tidy -p build examples/bank/include/bank/offline/lightweight_offline_queue.hpp
#     Error: no checks enabled.
#     $ echo $?
#     1
#
# against `6 warnings treated as errors` from the same command with the key in
# place. So the key is required and its absence is a red build with a message
# naming the problem, not a quietly unlinted directory. Nothing checks for the
# key, so keeping it here is a rule to hold by hand.
#
# ── Reach, stated rather than guessed ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This file is read when clang-tidy analyses a file under
# `examples/bank/include/` as a main file. For these headers that is the whole
# story: the root `HeaderFilterRegex` is `include/morph/.*`, which no example
# header matches, so a `TableName` reached from a .cpp is discarded as non-user
# code regardless of what this file says. The case that reaches CI is
# clang-tidy-diff analysing a *changed header* as a main file, which is the
# only way any of these 71 declarations is judged at all.
#
# The residue: a local variable or ordinary member spelled exactly `TableName`,
# written anywhere under examples/bank/include/ for some reason unrelated to
# the ORM, is not case-checked. Nothing else about it is suppressed, no other
# check is affected, and no other spelling is -- the probe above is the
# evidence for that rather than the intention.

InheritParentConfig: true

CheckOptions:
  - key:   readability-identifier-naming.VariableIgnoredRegexp
    value: '^TableName$'
